1
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:31,997
MATTHEW: Boca Raton, Florida. Home of the Florida Atlantic Owls. In 2011 the school opened a new football stadium. It borrowed more than forty-five million dollars to build it. And it needed somebody to put a name on it.

2
00:00:32,297 --> 00:00:42,841
MATTHEW: February 19, 2013. The trustees vote, and it is unanimous. Six million dollars over twelve years, the largest gift in school history. And the name on the building will be GEO Group Stadium.

3
00:00:43,141 --> 00:00:51,278
GARY MERCER: I am down here for the winter and I will say it. A donation is a donation. Every stadium in America has a bank or a pizza chain on it.

4
00:00:51,578 --> 00:00:57,612
FLORIDA ATLANTIC DESK: Gary, this one is not a pizza chain. GEO Group runs private prisons. Its headquarters is right here in Boca Raton.

5
00:00:57,912 --> 00:01:04,750
MATTHEW: The man who ran the company, George Zoley, had two degrees from FAU. He had once chaired the same board that took the money.

6
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:19,164
MATTHEW: Here is the part that was not in the press release. A federal judge in Mississippi had called one of the company's youth prisons a cesspool. Texas had shut down one of its youth centers over filthy conditions. And ten miles from campus, it ran a detention center for immigrants.

7
00:01:19,464 --> 00:01:27,039
FLORIDA ATLANTIC DESK: The day after the vote, somebody deleted the controversies section from the company's Wikipedia page. The account carried the name of a GEO spokesman.

8
00:01:27,339 --> 00:01:29,001
GARY MERCER: That is not a great look.

9
00:01:29,301 --> 00:01:40,652
MATTHEW: The students noticed. These were not football boosters. They were commuters and working students, and a lot of them were the children of immigrants. Within a day they had a name for the place. Owlcatraz.

10
00:01:40,952 --> 00:01:47,052
FLORIDA ATLANTIC DESK: Two days after the vote it was on the Colbert Report. Four days after that, more than thirty students were sitting in the president's office.

11
00:01:47,352 --> 00:01:50,921
MATTHEW: The president told a room of two hundred and fifty people that it was a done deal.

12
00:01:51,221 --> 00:01:55,196
FLORIDA ATLANTIC DESK: The Faculty Senate voted on it March 22. Twenty-five to nine against the name.

13
00:01:55,496 --> 00:02:03,197
MATTHEW: Then, on March 22, protesters surrounded the president's car. She drove off. And one of the protesters said the side mirror clipped them on the way out.

14
00:02:03,497 --> 00:02:05,124
GARY MERCER: She hit a student?

15
00:02:05,424 --> 00:02:09,951
FLORIDA ATLANTIC DESK: Allegedly clipped. The student said that by the book it was a hit and run. Nobody was charged.

16
00:02:10,251 --> 00:02:18,176
MATTHEW: Ten days later, on April first, the prison company pulled out. Its chief executive said the gift had surprisingly evolved into an ongoing distraction.

17
00:02:18,476 --> 00:02:23,887
MATTHEW: The president said, we'd like always to have good donors and keep our money. Six weeks later she resigned.

18
00:02:24,187 --> 00:02:28,032
GARY MERCER: All right. A donation is not just a donation. I take it back.

19
00:02:28,332 --> 00:02:34,864
MATTHEW: The kids won. No lawyers. No money. Six weeks and a nickname, and they sent a three billion dollar company home.

20
00:02:35,164 --> 00:02:46,184
MATTHEW: But listen to what they were actually saying. Their statement said the school was putting the families of its Hispanic students at risk of being detained. That was the fear in 2013. The detention business was getting too close to campus.

21
00:02:46,484 --> 00:02:56,958
MATTHEW: So hold that thought. Because twelve years later, and about fifty miles south, another Florida university got an offer. Not from the prison company this time. From the federal agency that fills its beds.

22
00:02:57,258 --> 00:03:00,422
MATTHEW: And that university did not hold a vote on a name. It signed.

23
00:03:04,722 --> 00:03:08,133
MATTHEW: That is part two. Boca Raton, Florida. Keep it real.
